Answer: theia is a static‑analysis technique that examines a deep‑learning program’s source code together with the properties of its training data. by building a library‑agnostic meta‑model of the program (capturing things such as dataset dimensions, layer counts, learning rates, etc.) and applying context‑sensitive analyses (call‑strings and parameter‑sensitive analyses) with a set of verification rules, theia automatically locates structural defects—errors in activation functions, layer configurations, loss definitions, preprocessing, missing or redundant layers, and similar issues. it works with keras and pytorch models and reports the bug’s location and a suggested fix early in the training process.

neuralint is an existing tool for detecting defects in deep‑learning programs. it also analyzes code to find bugs, but it focuses on a broader range of issues and, in comparative evaluations, identifies fewer structural problems than theia. it serves as a baseline against which theia’s effectiveness is measured.
